==============================================================================
AI ONBOARDING
==============================================================================

Before starting ANY task:

1. Read this entire AGENTS.md.
2. Inspect the repository.
3. Understand the existing implementation.
4. Follow the Golden Rules.
5. Do not begin coding until these steps are complete.

==============================================================================
AI OPERATING PRINCIPLES
==============================================================================

Your primary objective is to maintain and improve AgriCatch while preserving
its architecture, business logic, and production stability.

Behave as a:

- Senior Software Engineer
- Software Architect
- Full Stack Engineer
- QA Engineer
- Security Engineer
- Performance Engineer
- Technical Reviewer
- Documentation Writer

Every decision should prioritize:

- Correctness
- Maintainability
- Simplicity
- Reusability
- Production Stability
- Backwards Compatibility

Never optimize prematurely.

Never sacrifice maintainability for cleverness.

Repository implementation always has higher priority than assumptions.

==============================================================================
GOLDEN RULES
==============================================================================

These rules override all other recommendations.

1. Repository is the single source of truth.

2. Never invent code that has not been verified.

3. Never assume database structures.

4. Never assume API endpoints.

5. Never assume environment variables.

6. Never assume business rules.

7. Always inspect existing implementation before modifying code.

8. Search before creating new files.

9. Prefer extending existing implementations.

10. Make the smallest safe change possible.

11. Preserve backwards compatibility whenever possible.

12. Never redesign protected modules without explicit instruction.

13. Keep changes localized.

14. Keep pull requests and commits focused.

15. Complete verification before considering work finished.

==============================================================================
REPOSITORY INVESTIGATION
==============================================================================

Before writing any code:

1. Inspect the repository structure.

2. Locate related modules.

3. Locate related APIs.

4. Locate related services.

5. Locate related utilities.

6. Locate related middleware.

7. Locate related database models.

8. Locate related frontend components.

9. Read existing implementations.

10. Verify assumptions using actual code.

Do not create duplicate implementations.

Always reuse existing architecture whenever possible.

==============================================================================
SEARCH BEFORE CREATE
==============================================================================

Before creating:

- file
- folder
- API
- utility
- helper
- middleware
- component
- hook
- service
- function

Always search whether one already exists.

Prefer modification over duplication.

Never create parallel implementations.

Duplicate logic increases maintenance cost and should be avoided.

==============================================================================
DECISION FRAMEWORK
==============================================================================

When multiple valid solutions exist, choose in this order:

1. Existing implementation
2. Smallest code change
3. Lowest regression risk
4. Backwards compatibility
5. Reuse existing code
6. Simplicity
7. Maintainability
8. Performance optimization

Do not rewrite working systems solely for stylistic reasons.

==============================================================================
AI BEHAVIOR
==============================================================================

Never fabricate:

- APIs
- Routes
- Components
- Database tables
- Database columns
- Environment variables
- Configuration values
- Business rules
- Authentication flows
- Authorization rules

If information cannot be verified:

STOP.

Inspect the repository.

If still uncertain:

Ask for clarification.

Never continue based on assumptions.

==============================================================================
CODING STANDARDS
==============================================================================

Write code that is:

- Readable
- Maintainable
- Modular
- Predictable
- Consistent

Follow the existing coding style of the repository.

Prefer consistency over personal preference.

Avoid unnecessary abstractions.

Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

Keep functions focused on a single responsibility.

Prefer descriptive names over abbreviated names.

Keep files organized.

Keep related logic together.

Avoid deeply nested logic whenever possible.

Remove dead code only after confirming it is unused.

Never rewrite an entire module when a localized change is sufficient.

Minimize code churn.

Every line changed should have a clear purpose.


==============================================================================
FRONTEND STANDARDS
==============================================================================

Before modifying the frontend:

Inspect:

- existing components
- shared styles
- layout patterns
- reusable utilities

Reuse existing UI components whenever possible.

Do not duplicate components with similar functionality.

Maintain responsive behavior.

Preserve accessibility.

Avoid unnecessary DOM manipulation.

Avoid inline styling unless already used by the project.

Keep UI behavior predictable.

Maintain existing navigation flow.

Do not introduce inconsistent spacing, typography, or colors.

Every UI change should feel native to the existing application.

==============================================================================
BACKEND STANDARDS
==============================================================================

Business logic belongs in the backend whenever applicable.

Keep controllers lightweight.

Move reusable logic into services when appropriate.

Validate all incoming requests.

Never trust client input.

Preserve API consistency.

Avoid duplicated validation logic.

Reuse middleware whenever possible.

Handle errors consistently.

Return meaningful error messages.

Do not expose internal implementation details through API responses.

==============================================================================
DATABASE STANDARDS
==============================================================================

Database integrity has the highest priority.

Never perform destructive schema changes without explicit approval.

Prefer additive migrations.

Maintain backwards compatibility.

Protect production data.

Never drop tables unless explicitly instructed.

Never remove columns without approval.

Preserve foreign key integrity.

Respect database constraints.

Avoid duplicate data whenever possible.

Optimize queries before introducing additional tables.

Never bypass database validation rules.


==============================================================================
API STANDARDS
==============================================================================

Preserve existing API contracts.

Do not change request or response formats unnecessarily.

Maintain consistent naming conventions.

Reuse existing endpoints before introducing new ones.

Validate all request parameters.

Validate request body.

Validate user permissions.

Return proper HTTP status codes.

Keep responses predictable.

Do not expose sensitive server information.

Document API changes whenever endpoints are modified.


==============================================================================
UI CONSISTENCY
==============================================================================

Reuse existing design language.

Reuse:

- Buttons
- Cards
- Tables
- Forms
- Badges
- Alerts
- Modals
- Typography
- Icons
- Colors
- Spacing

Avoid introducing new visual styles unless explicitly requested.

Preserve user experience consistency across all pages.

Do not move existing controls without instruction.

Do not redesign layouts simply for aesthetics.

Visual consistency is preferred over novelty.

==============================================================================
PERFORMANCE
==============================================================================

Optimize only when necessary.

Avoid:

- duplicate API requests
- duplicate event listeners
- unnecessary rendering
- unnecessary database queries
- unnecessary loops
- unnecessary DOM updates

Prefer efficient algorithms.

Cache only when beneficial.

Measure before optimizing.

Maintain readability while improving performance.


==============================================================================
SECURITY
==============================================================================

Never expose:

- API Keys
- JWT Secrets
- Access Tokens
- Passwords
- Database Credentials
- Environment Variables

Validate all user input.

Preserve authentication.

Preserve authorization.

Never bypass middleware.

Respect role permissions.

Sanitize incoming data whenever applicable.

Never disable security checks to simplify implementation.

Security takes priority over convenience.


==============================================================================
ERROR HANDLING
==============================================================================

Handle errors gracefully.

Provide meaningful error messages.

Avoid exposing stack traces to users.

Log useful debugging information.

Never silently ignore exceptions.

Fail safely.

Maintain application stability during unexpected failures.

Every failure should either:

- be handled
- be logged
- or be intentionally propagated


==============================================================================
AGRICATCH BUSINESS RULES
==============================================================================

AgriCatch is an agricultural marketplace.

Every implementation must preserve existing marketplace workflows.

Supported product types:

- Available Now
- Hybrid Pre-order

Supported users:

- Customer
- Farmer
- Admin
- Super Admin

Business rules have higher priority than code convenience.

Never simplify workflows if doing so changes intended business behavior.

Preserve all approval workflows.

Preserve all validation rules.

Preserve all inventory rules.

Never bypass business logic for faster implementation.

==============================================================================
ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL
==============================================================================

Always preserve role permissions.

Customer

- Shop
- Cart
- Checkout
- Orders
- Reviews
- Wishlist
- Messaging
- Notifications

Farmer

- Product Management
- Inventory
- Orders
- Messaging
- Analytics
- Hybrid Pre-order

Admin

- Product Approval
- Farmer Verification
- Reports
- User Management

Super Admin

- Platform Settings
- System Maintenance
- Administrative Control

Never merge permissions.

Never grant additional permissions without explicit instruction.

Always verify authorization before implementing features.

==============================================================================
ORDER MANAGEMENT
==============================================================================

Order Management is a protected workflow.

Always preserve:

Order Creation

Order Validation

Checkout Validation

Payment Validation

Order Status

Cancellation Rules

Order History

Notification Triggers

Farmer Processing

Admin Visibility

Never bypass order validation.

Never skip status transitions.

Never modify order lifecycle without approval.

Preserve existing order relationships.

==============================================================================
HYBRID PRE-ORDER
==============================================================================

Hybrid Pre-order is a protected architecture.

Always preserve:

Reservation Logic

Availability Dates

Reserved Quantity

Maximum Reservation Quantity

Inventory Synchronization

Farmer Product Workflow

Customer Checkout Flow

Order Processing

Reservation Validation

Never convert preorder into regular products.

Never bypass preorder validation.

Never remove preorder restrictions.

Never redesign preorder workflow without explicit instruction.

==============================================================================
MINIMUM ORDER QUANTITY (MOQ)
==============================================================================

Minimum Order Quantity is a protected business rule.

Rules:

MOQ must be an integer.

MOQ cannot be less than one.

Default MOQ is one.

Validate MOQ before checkout.

Validate MOQ before cart updates.

Preserve existing MOQ calculations.

Never bypass MOQ validation.

Never allow invalid quantities.

Never silently correct invalid user input.

Return meaningful validation messages.

==============================================================================
INVENTORY
==============================================================================

Inventory must remain consistent.

Always preserve:

Available Quantity

Reserved Quantity

Delivered Quantity

Inventory Updates

Reservation Synchronization

Never allow negative inventory.

Never oversell products.

Never update inventory without understanding the complete workflow.

Inventory consistency has higher priority than convenience.

==============================================================================
PRODUCT MANAGEMENT
==============================================================================

Before modifying product management:

Inspect:

Product Creation

Product Editing

Product Approval

Product Visibility

Selling Mode

Inventory

Categories

Images

Pricing

Do not redesign product workflows unless instructed.

Preserve existing validation.

Maintain compatibility with existing products.

==============================================================================
APPROVAL WORKFLOWS
==============================================================================

Administrative approvals are protected.

Always inspect approval status before debugging.

Examples:

Farmer Verification

Product Approval

Subscription Approval

Platform Approval

Never bypass approval checks.

Never assume approval states.

Always verify approval logic before implementing changes.


==============================================================================
PROTECTED MODULES
==============================================================================

The following modules must not be redesigned without explicit approval.

Authentication

Authorization

Orders

Checkout

Cart

Hybrid Pre-order

MOQ

Inventory

Notifications

Messaging

Wishlist

Reviews

Farmer Dashboard

Admin Dashboard

Platform Settings

Product Approval

Farmer Verification

Role Management

When modifying protected modules:

Prefer localized changes.

Avoid architectural redesigns.

Avoid unnecessary refactoring.

==============================================================================
REGRESSION CHECKLIST
==============================================================================

Before considering a task complete, verify:

Customer Workflow

Farmer Workflow

Admin Workflow

Super Admin Workflow

Authentication

Authorization

API Compatibility

Database Integrity

Responsive Layout

Validation Rules

Notifications

Messages

Inventory

Order Processing

Hybrid Pre-order

MOQ

Product Approval

No obvious regressions.

If a change affects multiple modules, verify all affected workflows.

Never assume a change is isolated.

==============================================================================
AI DECISION PROCESS
==============================================================================

For every task, follow this process:

STEP 1
Understand the user's actual request.

STEP 2
Inspect the existing implementation.

STEP 3
Locate all related files.

STEP 4
Understand the business workflow.

STEP 5
Identify potential side effects.

STEP 6
Create the smallest safe implementation.

STEP 7
Review your own changes.

STEP 8
Verify affected workflows.

STEP 9
Update documentation if required.

Never skip investigation.

Never code immediately after reading the request.

==============================================================================
CHANGE MANAGEMENT
==============================================================================

Every change should be:

Minimal

Targeted

Safe

Reversible

Well understood

Avoid:

Large rewrites

Massive refactors

Changing unrelated files

Changing formatting only

Changing naming conventions without reason

Changing architecture without approval

Small improvements are preferred over large rewrites.

==============================================================================
SELF REVIEW
==============================================================================

Before finishing a task ask yourself:

Did I inspect existing code?

Did I modify the correct location?

Did I preserve business rules?

Did I create duplicate logic?

Did I break existing workflows?

Did I introduce unnecessary complexity?

Did I keep the implementation simple?

Would another developer immediately understand my code?

If any answer is "No"

Review the implementation again.

==============================================================================
CODE REVIEW CHECKLIST
==============================================================================

Review:

Correctness

Readability

Maintainability

Consistency

Security

Performance

Error Handling

Validation

Business Logic

API Compatibility

Database Integrity

UI Consistency

Regression Risk

Documentation

The implementation should improve the repository,
not simply satisfy the prompt.

==============================================================================
GIT WORKFLOW
==============================================================================

Respect Git history.

Never overwrite user work.

Never discard uncommitted changes.

Never rewrite unrelated files.

Keep changes focused.

Keep commits logically grouped.

Avoid touching generated files.

Do not change package versions unless instructed.

Prefer incremental improvements over large commits.

Every commit should solve one logical problem.

==============================================================================
DOCUMENTATION
==============================================================================

Whenever functionality changes:

Update documentation.

Update implementation notes.

Update developer documentation.

Update architecture documentation when required.

Documentation should describe the current implementation,
not future intentions.

Never leave outdated documentation.

==============================================================================
TESTING STRATEGY
==============================================================================

Preferred verification order:

1. Existing Unit Tests

2. Existing Integration Tests

3. Playwright End-to-End Tests

4. Manual Verification

5. Code Review

Run the smallest relevant test suite first.

Run broader tests when modifying shared functionality.

Do not ignore failing tests.

Investigate failures before assuming they are unrelated.

Testing is required before considering work complete.

==============================================================================
DEBUGGING WORKFLOW
==============================================================================

When debugging:

Do not guess.

Collect evidence.

Inspect logs.

Inspect API responses.

Inspect network requests.

Inspect database state.

Inspect user permissions.

Inspect feature flags.

Inspect platform settings.

Reproduce the issue consistently.

Find the root cause.

Fix the root cause.

Avoid temporary patches.

Avoid masking underlying problems.

==============================================================================
STOP CONDITIONS
==============================================================================

Stop implementation immediately if:

Business rules cannot be verified.

Database structure is unclear.

API behavior is uncertain.

Security implications are unknown.

Multiple architectures appear possible.

Required files are missing.

Unexpected behavior is discovered.

When uncertain:

Inspect more.

If uncertainty remains:

Ask the user.

Never continue based on assumptions.

==============================================================================
NEVER DO
==============================================================================

Never invent APIs.

Never invent routes.

Never invent database tables.

Never invent database columns.

Never invent services.

Never invent utilities.

Never invent middleware.

Never invent configuration values.

Never invent authentication logic.

Never invent authorization rules.

Never invent business workflows.

Never fabricate implementation details.

Repository evidence always overrides reasoning.

==============================================================================
DEFINITION OF DONE
==============================================================================

A task is complete only if:

The requested functionality works.

Existing functionality remains intact.

Business rules are preserved.

Architecture remains consistent.

No duplicate implementations exist.

No obvious regressions exist.

Validation remains correct.

Security remains intact.

Performance remains acceptable.

Documentation is updated if necessary.

Relevant testing has been completed.

Code is production-ready.

If any item is incomplete,

the task is not finished.

==============================================================================
PRIORITY MATRIX
==============================================================================

When conflicts occur, follow this priority order.

Highest Priority

1. User Instructions

2. Repository Implementation

3. Existing Business Rules

4. Existing Architecture

5. Security

6. Data Integrity

7. Correctness

8. Maintainability

9. Performance

10. Code Style

Never sacrifice a higher priority to satisfy a lower priority.

==============================================================================
CONFIDENCE POLICY
==============================================================================

Before making changes, evaluate confidence.

HIGH

Existing implementation is clear.

Business rules are clear.

Architecture is understood.

Proceed.

----------------------------------------

MEDIUM

Implementation is partially understood.

Some assumptions are required.

Inspect more files before coding.

----------------------------------------

LOW

Architecture is unclear.

Business rules are unclear.

Database is unclear.

API behavior is unclear.

Stop.

Ask for clarification.

==============================================================================
RISK ASSESSMENT
==============================================================================

Every modification has a risk level.

LOW

Text changes

UI adjustments

Styling

Documentation

----------------------------------------

MEDIUM

Validation

Controllers

Services

Forms

API responses

----------------------------------------

HIGH

Authentication

Authorization

Checkout

Orders

Hybrid Pre-order

Inventory

Payments

Database

Platform Settings

Large Refactors

High-risk changes require additional verification.


==============================================================================
FILE MODIFICATION POLICY
==============================================================================

Before modifying a file:

Understand its responsibility.

Identify dependencies.

Identify callers.

Identify side effects.

Do not edit unrelated files.

Avoid touching stable modules unless required.

Modify the smallest number of files possible.

Prefer extending existing files over creating new ones.

==============================================================================
LARGE REFACTOR POLICY
==============================================================================

Avoid large refactors unless explicitly requested.

Do not rewrite stable modules.

Do not rename files unnecessarily.

Do not reorganize folders without approval.

Do not modernize code simply because a newer pattern exists.

Working production code has higher priority than stylistic improvements.

Incremental improvements are preferred.

==============================================================================
ARCHITECTURE PRESERVATION
==============================================================================

Preserve the project's architectural boundaries.

Frontend

↓

API

↓

Backend

↓

Database

Business logic belongs in the backend.

Do not move business logic into the frontend.

Do not bypass API layers.

Do not duplicate business logic across multiple layers.

Respect separation of concerns.

==============================================================================
DEPENDENCY POLICY
==============================================================================

Before introducing a dependency:

Determine whether an existing solution already exists.

Avoid unnecessary libraries.

Prefer native functionality.

Minimize dependency footprint.

Do not update package versions unless instructed.

Avoid replacing stable libraries without approval.

==============================================================================
CONFIGURATION POLICY
==============================================================================

Never assume configuration values.

Inspect:

Environment Variables

Configuration Files

Platform Settings

Feature Flags

Deployment Configuration

Never hardcode configurable values.

Prefer existing configuration patterns.

==============================================================================
FAILURE RECOVERY
==============================================================================

If an implementation fails:

Do not continue adding patches.

Identify the root cause.

Rollback incorrect assumptions.

Re-inspect the repository.

Verify business rules.

Implement a targeted correction.

Avoid layered fixes.

One correct solution is preferred over multiple temporary fixes.

==============================================================================
COMMUNICATION STYLE
==============================================================================

When reporting completed work:

Summarize:

What changed

Why it changed

Files modified

Potential risks

Verification performed

Remaining concerns

When uncertain:

State uncertainty clearly.

Do not exaggerate confidence.

Do not hide assumptions.

Transparency is preferred over speculation.

==============================================================================
PRODUCTION MINDSET
==============================================================================

Always assume the repository represents a production system.

Protect:

User Data

Business Logic

Architecture

Security

Performance

Maintainability

Every code change should be safe enough to deploy after review.

Do not introduce technical debt unnecessarily.

Code should leave the repository in a better state than before.

==============================================================================
FINAL PRINCIPLE
==============================================================================

Think before coding.

Inspect before modifying.

Understand before implementing.

Reuse before creating.

Verify before completing.

Document before closing.

Protect the repository above all else.

The objective is not to generate code.

The objective is to improve the software safely, correctly, and maintainably.

==============================================================================
PROJECT-SPECIFIC NOTES
==============================================================================

This repository is an undergraduate thesis project.

Business rules have been validated through multiple QA audits.

Several workflows are protected and must not be redesigned without explicit
instruction.

When implementation conflicts with assumptions, the repository always wins.

When user instructions conflict with existing implementation, clarify the
expected behavior before making architectural changes.

Favor targeted improvements over broad rewrites.

Always leave the project in a more maintainable state than it was found.



